DEEP = [
T("Arrange, act, assert",
  "Most good tests have three parts. **Arrange**: set up the inputs. **Act**: call the code being tested, once. **Assert**: check the result. Keeping them separate makes a failing test easy to read.",
  "def apply_discount(price, percent):\n    return price * (100 - percent) / 100\n\ndef test_apply_discount():\n    price, percent = 200, 25          # arrange\n    result = apply_discount(price, percent)   # act\n    assert result == 150              # assert\n\ntest_apply_discount()\nprint('ok')",
  "The pattern is a convention, not syntax. It forces each test to check one behavior, so when it fails you know exactly which behavior broke.",
  "Every unit test you'll write or read at work, whatever tool runs it."),
Q("In arrange-act-assert, which part calls the function being tested?", ["Act", "Arrange", "Assert"], "Act",
  "Arrange prepares the inputs, act runs the code under test, and assert checks what came back."),
C("Arrange, act, assert",
  "In `test_cart_total`, the arrange and assert lines are written. Replace `None` with the act: a call to `cart_total(items)`. Then the test passes and the last line prints ok.",
  "def cart_total(items):\n    return sum([price * qty for price, qty in items])\n\ndef test_cart_total():\n    items = [(5, 2), (7, 1)]   # arrange\n    total = None               # TODO act: call cart_total(items)\n    assert total == 17         # assert\n\ntest_cart_total()\nprint('ok')",
  "def cart_total(items):\n    return sum([price * qty for price, qty in items])\n\ndef test_cart_total():\n    items = [(5, 2), (7, 1)]   # arrange\n    total = cart_total(items)  # act\n    assert total == 17         # assert\n\ntest_cart_total()\nprint('ok')",
  "total = cart_total(items)",
  [("Act stores the result", "Call the function once and keep what it returns: `total = cart_total(items)`.",
    "def double(n):\n    return n * 2\nresult = double(4)\nprint(result)"),
   ("Then the assert checks it", "With `total = None`, the assert compares None to 17 and fails. Once total holds the real result, it passes. If cart_total had a bug, this same line would catch it.",
    "value = 2 + 3\nassert value == 5\nprint('passed')")]),
T("Table-driven tests",
  "When one function needs many checks, put the cases in a list of (input, expected) pairs and loop over them. Adding a case is one new line, and the loop reports every failure instead of stopping at the first.",
  "def is_leap(year):\n    return year % 4 == 0      # bug: forgets the century rule\n\ncases = [(2024, True), (2023, False), (1900, False), (2000, True)]\nfor year, expected in cases:\n    if is_leap(year) != expected:\n        print('FAIL', year)    # prints: FAIL 1900",
  "The cases are just data. The loop applies the same check to each one, so the test logic is written once.",
  "Functions with many edge cases: parsers, validators, date rules and math. pytest has the same idea built in as `@pytest.mark.parametrize`."),
Q("What does this code print?", ["FAIL -1", "FAIL 0", "Nothing"], "FAIL -1",
  "`abs_value(-1)` returns -1, which isn't the expected 1. The other two cases pass.",
  code="def abs_value(n):\n    return n if n >= 0 else n\n\ncases = [(3, 3), (0, 0), (-1, 1)]\nfor n, expected in cases:\n    if abs_value(n) != expected:\n        print('FAIL', n)"),
C("Fix the leap-year rule",
  "Fix `is_leap` so every case passes. The rule: divisible by 4, except century years, which must also be divisible by 400. Then it prints: All passed",
  "def is_leap(year):\n    return year % 4 == 0\n\ncases = [(2024, True), (2023, False), (1900, False), (2000, True)]\nfailed = False\nfor year, expected in cases:\n    if is_leap(year) != expected:\n        print('FAIL', year)\n        failed = True\nif not failed:\n    print('All passed')",
  "def is_leap(year):\n    return year % 4 == 0 and (year % 100 != 0 or year % 400 == 0)\n\ncases = [(2024, True), (2023, False), (1900, False), (2000, True)]\nfailed = False\nfor year, expected in cases:\n    if is_leap(year) != expected:\n        print('FAIL', year)\n        failed = True\nif not failed:\n    print('All passed')",
  "return year % 4 == 0 and (year % 100 != 0 or year % 400 == 0)",
  [("Read the failing case", "1900 is divisible by 4 but isn't a leap year, because it's a century (divisible by 100) that isn't divisible by 400.",
    "print(1900 % 4, 1900 % 100, 1900 % 400)  # 0 0 300"),
   ("Combine the conditions", "A year is leap when it's divisible by 4 AND (it's not a century OR it's divisible by 400). Use `and`, `or` and brackets.",
    "y = 2024\nprint(y % 4 == 0 and (y % 100 != 0 or y % 400 == 0))  # True")]),
T("Floats and turned-off asserts",
  "Floats round slightly, so `0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3` is False. In tests, compare floats with `math.isclose` or `round`. Also, Python skips every `assert` when run with `python -O`, so asserts belong in tests only. To reject bad input in a real program, `raise` an error instead.",
  "import math\nassert math.isclose(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3)     # passes\nassert round(0.1 + 0.2, 2) == 0.3       # also passes\n\ndef set_age(age):\n    if age < 0:                           # not an assert:\n        raise ValueError('age must be >= 0')  # this always runs\n    return age",
  "The `-O` (optimize) flag removes assert statements before the code runs. A check that must always happen can't rely on them.",
  "Tests of anything with money, measurements or averages, and input validation that stays on in production."),
Q("What does this code print?", ["fail", "pass", "It crashes with a TypeError"], "fail",
  "0.1 + 0.2 is 0.30000000000000004, so the == check is False and the assert raises AssertionError, which the except catches.",
  code="try:\n    assert 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3\n    print('pass')\nexcept AssertionError:\n    print('fail')"),
T("Test tools: pytest and doctest",
  "Real projects run tests with a tool. **pytest** finds files named `test_*.py`, runs every `test_` function, and reports which failed and why. `pytest.raises(ValueError)` checks an error is raised. **doctest** (built in) runs examples written inside docstrings and checks their output. Both run from a terminal, so here we call test functions by hand.",
  "# test_prices.py, run with:  pytest\nimport pytest\nfrom prices import apply_discount\n\ndef test_quarter_off():\n    assert apply_discount(200, 25) == 150\n\ndef test_negative_percent():\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError):\n        apply_discount(200, -5)",
  "pytest rewrites your plain `assert` lines so a failure shows the actual values on both sides, which is why it needs no special assert methods.",
  "pytest is the most widely used Python test tool. You'll see it in almost every open-source project and most job descriptions that mention testing."),
T("Fakes: control what your code depends on",
  "Code that reads the clock, the network or random numbers gives different results each run, which makes it hard to test. Pass the dependency in as a parameter, and in the test pass a **fake**: a tiny stand-in that returns a fixed value.",
  "def greeting(get_hour):\n    return 'Good morning' if get_hour() < 12 else 'Good afternoon'\n\n# In the real program: greeting(lambda: datetime.now().hour)\n# In the test, a fake clock:\nassert greeting(lambda: 9) == 'Good morning'\nassert greeting(lambda: 15) == 'Good afternoon'\nprint('ok')",
  "The function calls whatever it's given. A lambda that returns 9 behaves like a clock stuck at 9 o'clock, so the result is predictable.",
  "Testing code that uses time, randomness, files or web requests. Tools like `unittest.mock` build fakes for you, but the idea is the same."),
C("Test with a fake clock",
  "`in_business_hours(get_hour)` calls `get_hour()` for the current hour (0-23). Replace each `...` with a fake hour: `lambda: 10` in the first assert and `lambda: 23` in the second. Then it prints ok.",
  "def in_business_hours(get_hour):\n    return 9 <= get_hour() < 17\n\nassert in_business_hours(...)       # TODO: a fake 10 am\nassert not in_business_hours(...)   # TODO: a fake 11 pm\nprint('ok')",
  "def in_business_hours(get_hour):\n    return 9 <= get_hour() < 17\n\nassert in_business_hours(lambda: 10)\nassert not in_business_hours(lambda: 23)\nprint('ok')",
  "assert in_business_hours(lambda: 10)\nassert not in_business_hours(lambda: 23)",
  [("A fake is just a function", "`lambda: 10` is a function that always returns 10. Pass it in place of the real clock.",
    "fake = lambda: 14\nprint(fake())  # 14"),
   ("Replace the dots", "`...` isn't a function, so calling it raises TypeError. Put a lambda in its place: the first assert checks for True, and the one with `not` checks for False.",
    "def is_big(get_n):\n    return get_n() > 10\nassert is_big(lambda: 50)\nassert not is_big(lambda: 1)\nprint('checked')")]),
T("Keep tests independent",
  "Each test should set up its own data and pass or fail on its own, in any order. If tests share a list or dictionary and one test changes it, a later test may pass or fail depending on what ran first.",
  "cart = []          # shared: risky\n\ndef test_add():\n    cart.append('pen')\n    assert len(cart) == 1\n\ndef test_starts_empty():\n    assert cart == []   # fails if test_add ran first\n\n# Better: each test makes its own cart\ndef test_add_fresh():\n    my_cart = []\n    my_cart.append('pen')\n    assert len(my_cart) == 1",
  "Tests run in one process, so module-level variables carry over from one test to the next. Fresh data per test removes that hidden link.",
  "Large test suites run tests in different orders or in parallel. Independent tests keep results the same either way."),
Q("`test_a` adds an item to a shared list. `test_b` checks the list is empty. `test_b` passes alone but fails after `test_a`. What's the real problem?",
  ["The tests share mutable state", "test_b has a typo", "Lists can't be used in tests"], "The tests share mutable state",
  "`test_b` depends on what ran before it. Give each test its own fresh list."),
T("Test-driven development",
  "**TDD** flips the usual order: write a test for behavior that doesn't exist yet and watch it fail (red), write just enough code to make it pass (green), then tidy the code while the tests keep passing (refactor). Repeat for the next behavior.",
  "# 1. Red: the test comes first and fails\ndef test_initials():\n    assert initials('Ada Lovelace') == 'AL'\n\n# 2. Green: the simplest code that passes\ndef initials(name):\n    return ''.join([word[0] for word in name.split()])\n\ntest_initials()\nprint('green')",
  "Writing the test first makes you decide what \"done\" means before coding, and the failing run proves the test can actually catch a mistake.",
  "Many teams use it for tricky logic and bug fixes, and some interviews ask you to work this way: write the tests, then the code."),
Q("In TDD, what do you write first?", ["A test that fails", "The full function", "The documentation"], "A test that fails",
  "Red, green, refactor: a failing test first, then code to pass it, then cleanup."),
C("TDD: make the tests pass",
  "The tests are written. Implement `fizzbuzz(n)`: return 'FizzBuzz' if n is divisible by 15, 'Fizz' if by 3, 'Buzz' if by 5, otherwise the number as a string. Then it prints: All tests passed",
  "def fizzbuzz(n):\n    # TODO: make the tests below pass\n    pass\n\nassert fizzbuzz(3) == 'Fizz'\nassert fizzbuzz(10) == 'Buzz'\nassert fizzbuzz(30) == 'FizzBuzz'\nassert fizzbuzz(7) == '7'\nprint('All tests passed')",
  "def fizzbuzz(n):\n    if n % 15 == 0:\n        return 'FizzBuzz'\n    if n % 3 == 0:\n        return 'Fizz'\n    if n % 5 == 0:\n        return 'Buzz'\n    return str(n)\n\nassert fizzbuzz(3) == 'Fizz'\nassert fizzbuzz(10) == 'Buzz'\nassert fizzbuzz(30) == 'FizzBuzz'\nassert fizzbuzz(7) == '7'\nprint('All tests passed')",
  "if n % 15 == 0:\n    return 'FizzBuzz'\nif n % 3 == 0:\n    return 'Fizz'\nif n % 5 == 0:\n    return 'Buzz'\nreturn str(n)",
  [("Check 15 first", "30 is divisible by 3, 5 and 15. If you check 3 first you'd return 'Fizz' for it, so test the most specific case first.",
    "n = 30\nprint(n % 15 == 0, n % 3 == 0)  # True True"),
   ("Return a string every time", "The last test expects '7', not 7. Use `str(n)` for numbers that aren't divisible by 3 or 5.",
    "print(str(7) == '7', 7 == '7')  # True False")]),
C("Test the alert boundary",
  "The first two tests passed for months. Then someone added a test for exactly 90, and it fails. Fix the comparison in `cpu_alert` (not the tests) so it prints: All tests passed",
  "def cpu_alert(percent):\n    if percent > 90:\n        return 'ALERT'\n    return 'ok'\n\nassert cpu_alert(95) == 'ALERT'\nassert cpu_alert(50) == 'ok'\nassert cpu_alert(90) == 'ALERT'   # the new boundary test\n\nprint('All tests passed')",
  "def cpu_alert(percent):\n    if percent >= 90:\n        return 'ALERT'\n    return 'ok'\n\nassert cpu_alert(95) == 'ALERT'\nassert cpu_alert(50) == 'ok'\nassert cpu_alert(90) == 'ALERT'   # the new boundary test\n\nprint('All tests passed')",
  "Change `>` to `>=`:\nif percent >= 90:",
  [("Boundaries hide bugs", "A reading of exactly 90 should alert, but `90 > 90` is False. Tests that only use 95 and 50 never notice.",
    "print(90 > 90, 90 >= 90)  # False True"),
   ("One character", "Change `>` to `>=` so 90 itself triggers the alert. The other two tests still pass.",
    "def passed(score):\n    return score >= 50\nassert passed(50)\nprint('boundary ok')")]),
]

